The results displayed in Tables \ref{tab:Entropyn10m5e1}--\ref{tab:Entropyn10m5e4} highlight that although the primal-dual interior point method typically requires fewer iterations than the parallel splitting method, this is not the case in this example. Notably, for error tolerances $\epsilon_1=10^{-3}$ and $\epsilon_2=10^{-4}$, our parallel splitting needs only $353$ iterations to deliver an optimal solution to the considered portfolio optimization problem, compared to the $470$ needed by the EVaR-PD method for $\epsilon_1$ (also $460$ versus $471$ for $\epsilon_2$). Furthermore, the parallel splitting method demonstrates faster computational times for all error tolerances. Specifically, our method that involves a parallel splitting algorithm converged 10 times faster than the primal-dual interior point method from \cite{AJF} for $\epsilon_1=10^{-3}$ and almost 9 as fast for $\epsilon_4=10^{-8}$, while both methods achieved comparable objective values.
